\section{How the runtime reaches a program}{

A short bootstrap header at the top of each generated program locates the
bundle root (the nearest directory at or above the program holding
\code{autoexec.R}) and sources \code{autoexec.R} into the program's own environment,
with \code{chdir = TRUE}. \code{autoexec.R} is the bundle's counterpart of a SAS
autoexec: generated by the translation, maintained by a person afterwards.
Its LIBRARIES section says where each libref reads from and writes to (a
relative path means inside the bundle folder); its LOAD section sources
\code{sas2r-helpers.R} (this runtime), resolves those paths with
\code{\link[=sas2r_resolve_registry]{sas2r_resolve_registry()}}, and sources \verb{_sas2r_formats.R} (the compiled
format catalog). Run a program with \code{source("<bundle>/<program>.R")}, or
with \code{Rscript} from any directory.

To use the runtime at the console, source the autoexec once --
\code{source("<bundle>/autoexec.R", chdir = TRUE)} -- and attach sas2r when you
want the help pages.
}

